        "message": "fix(22): arm64 archives were built for the HOST; plus the OCR fixture and a real chat bar\n\n--- 1. THE ONLY THING THAT SHIPS: arm64-v8a ---\n\n    ld: error: .../android-arm64-v8a/lib/libllama.a(llama.cpp.o)\n      is incompatible with aarch64linux\n\nDIAGNOSED AT THE ARCHIVE LEVEL, FROM THE GREEN JOB\n\nandroid-deps was green and assert_arch_member.sh had run on every archive and\npassed. The green run's own log says why it could not catch this:\n\n    -- The C compiler identification is Clang 14.0.7\n    -- Check for working C compiler:\n      /usr/local/lib/android/sdk/ndk/25.2.9519653/.../bin/clang - skipped\n\nBARE clang. The NDK's unprefixed clang is a host driver: it takes its target from\n--target and with none it builds for the build machine. So:\n\n    x86_64 host  --(bare clang, no --target)-->  x86_64 objects\n    labelled                                       arm64-v8a\n\nEvery archive in dist/lib was host-architecture. Nothing in the workflow noticed,\nbecause the toolchain file it hand-wrote was accepted:\n\n    set(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME Android)\n    set(CMAKE_ANDROID_ARCH_ABI arm64-v8a)\n\nand setting CMAKE_ANDROID_ARCH_ABI in a hand-rolled file is not the same thing as\nusing the NDK's file. Only the NDK's file emits --target=aarch64-linux-android24\nand selects aarch64-linux-android24-clang. Now used directly:\n\n    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=\"$ANDROID_NDK_HOME/build/cmake/android.toolchain.cmake\"\n    -DANDROID_ABI=${{ matrix.abi }}\n    -DANDROID_PLATFORM=android-${{ env.API }}\n\nAND THE DRIVER IS NOW ASSERTED, because the archives check proved insufficient:\n\n    EXPECT=\"${{ matrix.target }}24-clang\"\n    GOT=$(grep -aoE '[a-z0-9_]+-linux-android[0-9]+-clang' build.log | head -1)\n    [ \"$GOT\" = \"$EXPECT\" ] || { echo \"::error::CMake selected '$GOT' ...\"; exit 1; }\n\nCMake naming the wrong compiler is UPSTREAM of the archives and costs one line of\noutput to catch, instead of an hour of compiling and a cross-link error from a\ndifferent job twenty minutes later.\n\n--- 2. THE OCR FIXTURE ---\n\n    java.lang.AssertionError: fixture image missing at\n      /storage/emulated/0/Android/data/com.grapsee.gsai/files/Pictures/invoice.png\n\n`grep -c invoice.png` on the script returned 0. Nothing ever created it, so a2\nSKIPPED -- and a skip hides the missing input rather than naming it.\n\nNow generated on the runner with ImageMagick and pushed to the exact path the test\nreads. GENERATED, NOT COMMITTED: a committed PNG is a binary in the repository and\nthe marker would be a constant in two places free to drift. Drawing it here means\nthe string the test asserts and the string in the image are the same edit. If\nImageMagick is absent the step says so, so the test skips with a reason instead of\nsilently.\n\n--- 3. A NON-EMPTY STRING IS NOT A REPLY ---\n\na1 PASSED on this output:\n\n    chat -> , i have a question about the following code:\n    ```\n    #include <iostream>\n\nThat is the PROMPT echoed back -- the prompt template is not being applied to the\nmodel's output at all. A non-empty check cannot tell a reply from a prompt\nfragment, so the bar is now three properties:\n\n    out.length > 20                              a fragment this short is not an answer\n    not a substring of the prompt                it is not the prompt\n    contains one of the/ is/ a/ i/ you/ hello/...  it is natural language\n\nIf the model returns prompt fragments the sampler or the prompt template is wrong,\nand these three now fail loudly on exactly that instead of passing.",
